---
/**
 * The deliverables under a stage, as disclosures rather than bullets.
 *
 * ## Why this is not DetailList
 *
 * `DetailList` renders the short lists elsewhere on the site, which are four
 * or five items with nothing behind them. These are four, six and eight, one
 * under another down a single page, and on a phone they were a wall of
 * wrapping lines nobody scans. Each row now shows the deliverable and opens
 * to the qualifier.
 *
 * `DetailList` is untouched. Three other call sites use it and none of them
 * wanted this.
 *
 * ## Why `<details>` and no JavaScript
 *
 * Because everything it needs is already in the element. It opens without a
 * script, so it works on a page whose JavaScript failed; the summary is a
 * button to the keyboard and announced as expanded or collapsed to a screen
 * reader, with no ARIA to get wrong; and the browser's own find-in-page opens
 * a closed row to show a match, which no hand-built accordion does.
 *
 * A row with no `body` is not a disclosure at all, just a line. Wrapping it in
 * a `<details>` with nothing inside would give the reader a control that opens
 * onto nothing.
 *
 * ## Open above 800px, closed below
 *
 * 800px is where `.split` stacks. Above it the list sits in a column beside
 * the stage heading with room to spare and there is nothing to gain by hiding
 * eight lines; below it the column is the whole width and the list was the
 * wall this replaced.
 *
 * The rows are open in the markup and a script closes them, never the other
 * way round. The two are not equivalent: whatever happens to the script, the
 * state it fails into is everything visible. It is inline and sits directly
 * after the list so it runs while the parser is still here, before this part
 * of the page has painted, rather than expanded and then collapsing.
 *
 * It runs once. Crossing 800px afterwards, by resizing or by turning a phone,
 * leaves the rows as they are: someone who has opened four of them has said
 * something about what they want to read, and a resize is not a reason to
 * throw that away.
 */
interface Props {
  items: { label: string; body?: string }[];
}
const { items } = Astro.props;
---

<ul class="detail detail--open">
  {
    items.map((item) =>
      item.body ? (
        <li class="row--opens">
          <details open>
            <summary>
              <span class="detail-label" set:html={item.label} />
              <span class="detail-mark" aria-hidden="true" />
            </summary>
            <p set:html={item.body} />
          </details>
        </li>
      ) : (
        <li>
          <span class="detail-label" set:html={item.label} />
        </li>
      ),
    )
  }
</ul>

<script is:inline>
  (function () {
    var list = document.currentScript.previousElementSibling;
    if (!list || !window.matchMedia) return;
    /* 800px is the `.split` stacking breakpoint, in one place in the CSS and
       one here. If that moves, this moves with it. */
    if (!matchMedia('(max-width: 800px)').matches) return;
    var rows = list.querySelectorAll('details[open]');
    for (var i = 0; i < rows.length; i++) rows[i].open = false;
  })();
</script>
